a09f76b5-a170-b338-3926-3059f28c105d/13-0
00:00:36.198 --> 00:00:43.038
<v Vikram Iyer>Sure. So at 14:02 on Thursday we shipped payments-api 3.12. Around 14:20 traffic picked up, the autoscaler added</v>

a09f76b5-a170-b338-3926-3059f28c105d/13-1
00:00:43.238 --> 00:00:49.318
<v Vikram Iyer>pods, and by 14:31 checkout started returning 502s. We were fully down for about thirty-eight minutes.</v>

a09f76b5-a170-b338-3926-3059f28c105d/13-2
00:00:49.518 --> 00:00:51.418
<v Vikram Iyer>The rollback finished at 15:09.</v>

3898d190-f9eb-dacc-0cb1-e29c658cda14/14-0
00:00:52.443 --> 00:00:57.003
<v Priya Raman>And the root cause is basically my change. 3.12 created a new</v>

3898d190-f9eb-dacc-0cb1-e29c658cda14/14-1
00:00:57.203 --> 00:01:03.283
<v Priya Raman>Redis connection pool per request handler instead of sharing one per pod, so every pod opened</v>

3898d190-f9eb-dacc-0cb1-e29c658cda14/14-2
00:01:03.483 --> 00:01:06.143
<v Priya Raman>roughly four times more connections than before.</v>

8a6a63ec-24ed-e6a4-6b4c-b2424a23d596/15-0
00:01:06.685 --> 00:01:13.525
<v Vikram Iyer>Right, and when the autoscaler went from twelve pods to thirty, we blew straight past Redis maxclients. Redis</v>

8a6a63ec-24ed-e6a4-6b4c-b2424a23d596/15-1
00:01:13.725 --> 00:01:18.665
<v Vikram Iyer>started refusing connections, and checkout doesn't have any fallback, so it just failed.</v>

1a61dbe2-2e44-158b-ae97-ba94d0eda82f/16-0
00:01:19.271 --> 00:01:24.591
<v Alex Moreno>Which is also why the retries made it worse. Every refused connection retried three</v>

1a61dbe2-2e44-158b-ae97-ba94d0eda82f/16-1
00:01:24.791 --> 00:01:26.311
<v Alex Moreno>times with no backoff.</v>

b64ce422-8c38-fb29-18f1-35d25f557203/17-0
00:01:27.334 --> 00:01:33.794
<v Maya Chen>Okay. No blame here, the change went through review and none of us caught it. What matters</v>

